00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:10.810
COVID-19—that’s the answer.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:16.420
That’s what caused all the shortages of
2020 and 2021.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:17.420
That’s it.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:21.550
COVID-19 caused the shortages in the same
way that an iceberg caused the Titanic to

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:26.140
sink… or the collapse of Lehman Brothers
caused the Great Recession… or the straw

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:27.760
broke the camel’s back.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:33.170
That’s really to say, COVID did cause these
shortages—they would not have happened in

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:37.109
the manner they did, when they did, if not
for the pandemic.

00:00:37.109 --> 00:00:41.420
But just as the Titanic’s design flaws would
eventually lead to calamity, the vulnerabilities

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:45.170
in the global economy would eventually lead
to a recession, and something would eventually

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:51.010
break the overloaded camel’s back, COVID
caused these shortages, but it’s not what

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.239
made them possible.

00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:59.229
What made them possible is a fundamental,
structural vulnerability in the way today’s

00:00:59.229 --> 00:01:01.420
supply chains work.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:08.350
On April 13th, 2021, Boba Bliss, a small bubble
tea shop in Dublin, California, posted on

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:09.350
Facebook.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:12.970
They explained that they were having a hard
time getting their ingredients, and that this

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.420
would mean their menu would have to change
daily—reflective of what they could actually

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:17.960
get from their supplier.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:23.399
On April 15th, MighTea Boba, in Canandaigua,
New York explained on Facebook that they would

00:01:23.399 --> 00:01:27.990
have to serve a smaller quantity of tapioca
pearls in each drink, and would no longer

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:32.270
be offering extra as an option, due to a shortage
of this ingredient.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:37.289
Bing’s Boba Tea in Tucson, Arizona said
much the same one week later, and Tea Hut

00:01:37.289 --> 00:01:40.909
in San Francisco, California then announced
that they would have to raise their prices

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:44.909
due to a 26% rise in their ingredient costs.

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:50.380
All across the country, shops selling this
popular Taiwanese drink simply could not get

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.909
ingredients in the quantities demanded, despite
no dramatic increase in demand.

00:01:55.909 --> 00:02:00.869
However, thousands of miles away, in Taiwan,
where much of the world’s Bubble Tea ingredients

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:04.409
are made, things were operating fairly normally.

00:02:04.409 --> 00:02:08.580
With single-digit daily case numbers for much
of COVID’s reign, factories on the island

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:14.200
were able to work at full-force in a way they
couldn’t elsewhere but still, somehow, Boba

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.940
Bliss, MighTea Boba, Bing’s Boba Tea, Tea
Hut, and thousands of other shops across the

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.730
US couldn't get their product.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:27.070
The problem had less to do with anything happening
in Taiwan, and more with what what happening

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:31.240
here—in the waters off the coast of Los
Angeles.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:36.860
Throughout the first half of 2021, a rotating
cast of dozens of cargo ships have dotted

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.430
the Pacific’s horizon.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:44.010
Each sat there, at anchor, sometimes for weeks,
until a spot opened for them at the port of

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.430
Los Angeles or Long Beach.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:47.840
This is abnormal.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:52.590
While the year prior it was possible for a
ship or two to have to wait a day or two,

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:56.790
it was never dozens of ships for dozens of
days.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:01.401
Transit time from Taiwan to Los Angeles typically
takes about two weeks, so an additional ten

00:03:01.401 --> 00:03:05.660
days of waiting represents a near doubling
of shipping times.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:11.210
The root cause of this was, of course, a perfect,
pandemic-fueled storm.

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:16.890
As the 2020 holiday season approached, more
people were getting back to work in the US,

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:21.430
consumer spending was ticking up, and online
shopping was exploding.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:26.210
With limited access to travel, restaurants,
shows, and other services, physical products

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:30.440
were one of the few ways people could spend
their discretionary income.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:36.080
More goods than ever were coming from factories
in Asia, and 49.1% of those imports to the

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:42.430
US passed through one of just two ports—Los
Angeles and Long Beach.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:48.440
Compared to a year prior, before COVID hit
the US in earnest, February 2021 saw a 31%

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:53.500
increase in ships and a 49% increase in container
traffic at these two ports.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:56.920
They just couldn’t keep up.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:02.350
Simultaneously, however, a significant tranche
of dock-workers were constantly out sick due

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:06.140
to COVID itself or the quarantine procedures
it demanded.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.750
But the disorder doesn’t end there.

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:15.380
While a year ago a 20-foot container would
cost about $1,800, they now run for $3,500.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:20.750
There’s a regional shortage of shipping
containers that’s having global consequences.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:26.830
You see, in 2020, just as much of the world
entered lockdowns, China’s manufacturing

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:29.040
industry was emerging from it.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.010
The entire world bought masks, personal protective
equipment, and regular goods from the country

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:38.420
and once those containers arrived in ports
like Long Beach, they were unloaded.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:42.790
However, due to those destination country’s
lockdowns, there wasn’t really much that

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:45.970
needed to be shipped out.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:50.860
For every 100 containers that are imported
into the US, only 40 are exported.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:55.760
So, containers stacked up in the places there
weren’t needed, because they weren’t needed,

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:59.120
and they didn’t make it back to the places
where they were, like Asia.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.940
In the case of the ports of Los Angeles and
Long Beach, their capacity crunch means they

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:07.900
don’t even have the time to justify loading
ships with empty containers for return to

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:09.190
Asia.

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:13.370
Not only that, but a shortage of truck drivers
in the US means that it’s tough to actually

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:18.150
get empty containers back from their final
destinations to ports to send them back to

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:19.150
Asia.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:23.990
So, a shortage a shipping containers is worsening
a shortage of shipping capacity, which is

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:28.030
worsened by a shortage of port capacity, which
is worsening the shortage of shipping containers,

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:32.010
which itself is worsened by a shortage of
truck drivers, all of which is causing a shortage

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.330
of bubble tea ingredients.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:39.980
Shortages causing shortages causing shortages—its
a vicious cycle that is only worsening as

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:42.780
economies reopen.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:46.930
Across the world, there’s a proliferation
of scarcity.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:51.780
Lumber prices in the US quadrupled after predicted
decline failed to manifest, and a boom in

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:54.620
construction and renovation did instead.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:59.760
The use and construction of pools also accelerated,
as outdoor space became more valuable, spurring

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.740
increased demand for chlorine which, when
mixed with reduced supply as a result of a

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:09.570
fire at a major chlorine plant in Louisiana,
led to sky-high prices, and insufficient availability.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:14.680
As restaurants pivoted to take-out and delivery,
bulk ketchup was out, and single-serve packets

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:18.690
were in, but with manufacturers unwilling
to spend significantly to increase production

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:23.139
capacity for a short-term bump, the packets
became scarce, and companies started successfully

00:06:23.139 --> 00:06:26.750
selling them for $10 per 50 on eBay.

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:32.060
However, the greatest shortage of all can
be seen here, in the empty parking lots of

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.280
Anchorage, Alaska’s airport.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:40.600
The state relies heavily on its summer tourism
season, and after missing it last year, 2021’s

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:41.850
season is crucial.

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:46.460
Facing uncertainty about the legality of their
operation until May, cruise companies cancelled

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:51.910
many of their summer sailings to Alaska, so
this year is all about independent, domestic

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:53.350
tourists.

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:58.300
To traverse the vast wilderness of the last
frontier, independent travelers need rental

00:06:58.300 --> 00:06:59.300
cars.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:04.580
However, these travelers can’t get rental
cars—regardless of which date you search

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:08.240
in summer 2021, there simply isn’t availability.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:15.050
For the lucky few who do find one, current
market rates are over $500 a day for a compact

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:16.050
car.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:21.220
American travelers can now legally, safely,
and easily get to Alaska, but they can’t

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:25.180
get around Alaska, which means that eager
travelers aren’t going.

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:30.520
You see, during the depths of the pandemic,
Hertz, Enterprise, National, and other rental

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:35.650
car companies sold off much of their fleets
of cars—they needed money somehow, and it

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:37.590
wasn’t coming from rentals themselves.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:44.449
Now, however, with vaccinations easily available
to all in the US, there is demand for rentals,

00:07:44.449 --> 00:07:45.750
yet no supply.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:50.979
In Alaska especially, with its highly seasonal
tourism market, its normal for the rental

00:07:50.979 --> 00:07:55.020
car companies to sell off their old vehicles
in the fall, and purchase new ones in spring,

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:59.681
but the latter half of that didn’t happen
this year, because there’s a massive, global

00:07:59.681 --> 00:08:05.169
shortage of new cars, and manufacturers are
simply unable to fulfill the huge orders rental

00:08:05.169 --> 00:08:07.270
car companies are asking for.

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:12.990
You see, in Spring, 2020, as COVID took hold
of the globe, car manufacturers cancelled

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:17.540
and reduced their orders for components as
they anticipated reduced demand for new vehicles.

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:22.930
They were right on that—demand did go down
as it tends to during any recession—but

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:26.080
it recovered faster than anyone imagined.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:30.830
Regardless, the plan was that, once recovery
started, they’d boost component orders and

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:32.649
manufacturing in step.

00:08:32.649 --> 00:08:36.909
The problem was that they’re now at the
back of the line, and for one, critical component

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:41.879
of modern automobiles, there’s quite a long
line.

00:08:41.879 --> 00:08:44.540
Computer chips are sold out everywhere.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:48.410
It was the perfect storm of circumstances.

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:53.879
First, the US’ trade war with China caused
the country’s manufacturers to start stockpiling

00:08:53.879 --> 00:08:54.930
the chips.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:59.370
Then, COVID led to production shutdowns in
certain areas of the world, demand for consumer

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:03.970
electronics exploded as people spent more
time at home, and now, even the solution is

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:09.569
causing problems as silicon is both needed
in computer chips and vaccine vials.

00:09:09.569 --> 00:09:14.389
Altogether, the reasons are complex, but the
result is clear: this is why you still can’t

00:09:14.389 --> 00:09:18.970
easily buy a PS5, this is why Samsung likely
won’t release a new Galaxy smartphone this

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:22.680
year, and this is why you can’t get a rental
car in Alaska.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:29.130
In a twist of cruel irony, the car industry
is culpable for all of this.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:34.350
That’s because, decades ago, the very industry
that is now suffering the hardest propagated

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:38.540
a system that made shortages such as this
so possible.

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:45.389
Eighty years ago, as Japan emerged from World
War Two, Toyota had a problem.

00:09:45.389 --> 00:09:49.850
The world was marching towards further globalization,
and Japan especially was starting to develop

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:52.180
a trade relationship with the US.

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:59.279
American car manufacturers, at the time, though,
had a huge cost edge over their Japanese counterparts.

00:09:59.279 --> 00:10:03.459
Especially during the post-war economic boom,
demand for their vehicles was so high that

00:10:03.459 --> 00:10:07.869
American manufacturers had incredible economies
of scale.

00:10:07.869 --> 00:10:12.720
Their process was batch manufacturing—at
a given time, a given assembly line would

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:17.019
build one type of one model of one vehicle,
exclusively.

00:10:17.019 --> 00:10:22.649
Then, they’d switch to another type of another
model, and so on and so forth.

00:10:22.649 --> 00:10:27.959
With this, the manufacturers could use massive
production lines working at breakneck pace,

00:10:27.959 --> 00:10:29.259
unlike Toyota’s.

00:10:29.259 --> 00:10:32.550
Theirs were small and slow.

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:36.800
The domestic car market in Japan was limited,
and it demanded quite a lot of variety in

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:38.390
vehicle types.

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:42.550
This meant that they didn’t have the scale
to make batch manufacturing work well—at

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:44.750
least as well as in the US.

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:49.079
Their inability to realize the gains of batch
manufacturing meant that they simply would

00:10:49.079 --> 00:10:53.940
have to have higher prices compared to their
new American competitors, but there just wasn’t

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:55.779
a clear alternative.

00:10:55.779 --> 00:10:58.389
So, they created one.

00:10:58.389 --> 00:11:02.069
They created the Toyota Production System.

00:11:02.069 --> 00:11:08.189
It had two core principles, starting with
autonomation—not automation, autonomation.

00:11:08.189 --> 00:11:12.399
This was their term for the efficient use
of automation.

00:11:12.399 --> 00:11:17.839
Now, complete automation would require machines
to build cars, plus diagnose any problems,

00:11:17.839 --> 00:11:22.910
plus fix those problems, but having machines
fix their own problems is very complicated

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:24.879
and very expensive.

00:11:24.879 --> 00:11:29.690
So, in their autonomation system, they look
for only what machines can do better than

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:34.561
humans, which in their context was most of
vehicle assembly and problem diagnosis, while

00:11:34.561 --> 00:11:38.220
problem-solving was left to humans.

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:43.449
Autonomation was and still is a crucial, critical
component of the Toyota Production System,

00:11:43.449 --> 00:11:48.339
but it wasn’t the core principle that would
go on to change manufacturing fundamentally.

00:11:48.339 --> 00:11:52.829
What did was the principle of just-in-time
manufacturing.

00:11:52.829 --> 00:11:57.720
All it really is is this: each step in the
manufacturing process should end when the

00:11:57.720 --> 00:11:59.110
next one is ready to start.

00:11:59.110 --> 00:12:02.879
It’s a pull system, as opposed to a push
system.

00:12:02.879 --> 00:12:07.110
Just as your Coffee shop doesn’t start making
your latte until you ask for it, the polyvinyl

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:11.139
butyral resin should only leave the chemicals
plant when the laminated safety glass production

00:12:11.139 --> 00:12:15.100
facility is ready to start making windshields,
and it should only start making windshields

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:18.540
when the final assembly line is ready to put
all the pieces together, and it should only

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:21.850
start putting all the pieces together when
the dealership is ready to put more cars on

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:23.129
its lot.

00:12:23.129 --> 00:12:28.050
In the view of the Toyota Production System,
excess inventory is waste, because holding

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:32.240
inventory costs money without making money.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:36.589
Elimination of excess inventory is elimination
of waste, and elimination of waste is what

00:12:36.589 --> 00:12:39.459
leads to production efficiency.

00:12:39.459 --> 00:12:40.869
This system worked.

00:12:40.869 --> 00:12:45.559
In the early 1950’s, Toyota was on the brink
of bankruptcy.

00:12:45.559 --> 00:12:50.639
Today, the company is the largest auto manufacturer
in the world, and the principles of the Toyota

00:12:50.639 --> 00:12:55.209
Production System can be found in any business
textbook.

00:12:55.209 --> 00:12:59.199
Just-in-time manufacturing is no longer an
innovation—it’s the norm.

00:12:59.199 --> 00:13:02.980
Articles aren’t written about companies
implementing just-in-time—they’re written

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:05.279
about those that don’t.

00:13:05.279 --> 00:13:09.149
In fact, just-in-time has become even more
integral today.

00:13:09.149 --> 00:13:14.309
If there are six steps in a manufacturing
process and each holds two months of inventory,

00:13:14.309 --> 00:13:18.600
it would take a year to pivot to producing
a new product.

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:24.670
The short product life-cycles of today’s
technology industry would not accept that.

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:28.959
Just-in-time is such a simple principle, but
the pursuit of the elimination of waste is

00:13:28.959 --> 00:13:33.260
now the central mission of any major manufacturer.

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:38.279
However, most did it wrong.

00:13:38.279 --> 00:13:43.660
Manufacturers globally saw the headline—elimination
of inventory leads to massive efficiency gains—and

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:47.160
jumped on that without actually determining
what made it work for Toyota.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:51.679
They ignored that Japan’s small physical
size made for short domestic supply chains,

00:13:51.679 --> 00:13:53.879
less vulnerable to things going wrong.

00:13:53.879 --> 00:13:58.420
They ignored the company’s production leveling—finding
the average daily demand and producing that,

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:01.119
regardless of short-term changes in demand.

00:14:01.119 --> 00:14:05.560
They ignored the fact that eliminating excess
inventory is different from eliminating all

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:06.639
inventory.

00:14:06.639 --> 00:14:11.149
They ignored the principle of growing strong
teams of cross-functional workers, predicted

00:14:11.149 --> 00:14:12.639
on respecting people.

00:14:12.639 --> 00:14:18.069
They ignored the culture of stopping and fixing
problems, to get things right the first time.

00:14:18.069 --> 00:14:23.029
They ignored huge swaths of the Toyota Way
and created a system that’s less effective

00:14:23.029 --> 00:14:28.369
and less resilient, but can impress shareholders
through short-term savings.

00:14:28.369 --> 00:14:33.970
How Toyota has effectively implemented this
system fills books, but many are just reading

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:35.949
the covers.

00:14:35.949 --> 00:14:39.670
Even Toyota, though, is not perfect.

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:45.269
In 2011, Japan was rocked by a 9.0 magnitude
earthquake—the fourth strongest ever recorded

00:14:45.269 --> 00:14:46.720
anywhere.

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:51.629
Not only did this cause immense destruction
to life and property, but it also led Toyota

00:14:51.629 --> 00:14:55.190
to recognize a flaw in its own system.

00:14:55.190 --> 00:15:00.259
As Japan recovered, some supply chains were
quick to as well—for example, securing plastic

00:15:00.259 --> 00:15:03.589
resin for door panel production is not difficult.

00:15:03.589 --> 00:15:08.439
There are plenty of manufacturers globally
creating easily substitutable alternatives.

00:15:08.439 --> 00:15:13.350
That’s not the case with, say, semiconductors.

00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:18.269
The hugely expensive facilities that create
these chips require years to construct, and

00:15:18.269 --> 00:15:23.999
after the 2011 earthquake, it took many months
to mend them back to operating status.

00:15:23.999 --> 00:15:30.109
This surfaced a truth that had never been
fully considered—not all supply chains are

00:15:30.109 --> 00:15:31.829
made equal.

00:15:31.829 --> 00:15:35.379
Plastic resin can handle supply chain disruption.

00:15:35.379 --> 00:15:37.009
Semiconductors cannot.

00:15:37.009 --> 00:15:41.170
Therefore, Toyota made changes.

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:46.749
All along, their mission was not to eliminate
inventory full-stop—it was to eliminate

00:15:46.749 --> 00:15:48.670
excess inventory.

00:15:48.670 --> 00:15:51.070
Supply chain disruption is inevitable.

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:54.879
It’s inevitable in the same way that Titanic
flawed design would eventually encounter an

00:15:54.879 --> 00:15:59.809
iceberg or the structural economic vulnerabilities
of 2008 would eventually collide with a market

00:15:59.809 --> 00:16:01.089
panic.

00:16:01.089 --> 00:16:06.850
Therefore, semiconductor inventory is not
excess because inevitably, due to the inevitability

00:16:06.850 --> 00:16:13.999
of disruption, excess semiconductor inventory
will eventually become necessary.

00:16:13.999 --> 00:16:18.579
Recognizing this, Toyota, in recent years,
has started to build up a stockpile of two

00:16:18.579 --> 00:16:23.249
to six months worth of chips, and that’s
why the company is the only major vehicle

00:16:23.249 --> 00:16:28.350
manufacturer that is unfazed by the semiconductor
shortage.

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:30.949
Toyota followed its own principles.

00:16:30.949 --> 00:16:34.239
It did not stray from them, and it did not
reinvent them.

00:16:34.239 --> 00:16:38.889
It’s no surprise that Toyota excels at implementing
its own system, but it is a surprise that

00:16:38.889 --> 00:16:45.550
the entire manufacturing world has so wholeheartedly
embraced flawed implementation of the system.

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:50.389
The reality is that just-in-time manufacturing
is more efficient, but some think that that

00:16:50.389 --> 00:16:53.899
efficiency must come with a trade-off in resilience.

00:16:53.899 --> 00:16:56.879
That’s simply not true.

00:16:56.879 --> 00:17:02.319
Proper just-in-time supply chains must inherently
be inflexible once formed, but their form

00:17:02.319 --> 00:17:03.560
is flexible.

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:07.429
It’s a philosophy, not an equation.

00:17:07.429 --> 00:17:12.750
Shortages in an economy are incredibly complex,
and any explanation of them is an oversimplification,

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:16.809
but just-in-time manufacturing does not have
to be the cause.

00:17:16.809 --> 00:17:21.709
In addition, disruption is inevitable, so
it can’t be the cause.

00:17:21.709 --> 00:17:25.829
You can’t blame the fact that your house
is sinking on the fact that you built it in

00:17:25.829 --> 00:17:27.199
the ocean.

00:17:27.199 --> 00:17:33.480
A ruthless pursuit of short-term profit at
the expense of long-term gain is the cause.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:38.740
Running as close as possible to the edge is
the most efficient manner to manufacture,

00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:40.480
until it isn’t.

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:45.200
Constructing a resilient supply chain requires
long-term thinking, but most companies have

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:48.870
not nurtured an environment that allows for
that.

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:54.100
As the world recovers from the pandemic, many
will ditch what they call just-in-time manufacturing,

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:58.820
as the alternative is to accept that the very
people in charge of those companies have’t

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:00.620
implemented it properly.

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:07.080
To be fair, few have, and even Toyota is still
learning how to best implement its own system.

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:12.559
However, Toyota has proven that strict adherence
to its principles means just-in-time manufacturing

00:18:12.559 --> 00:18:18.360
can be everything at once—it can be both
efficient and resilient, if one knows what

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:20.680
is truly excess.

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:25.260
Proper implementation of the Toyota system
won’t fix every shortage but it did fix

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:27.940
one shortage for one company.

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:32.080
Therefore, if the auto industry had built
a stockpile of critical components, if it

00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:36.190
had implemented production leveling more strictly,
and if it had understood vulnerabilities in

00:18:36.190 --> 00:18:42.169
inbound supply chains, Alaska would have its
rental cars.

